  q(MSK,'Early after a rotator cuff repair, which exercise is usually allowed?','Passive range of motion within limits set by the surgeon',['Heavy overhead lifting','Push-ups','Throwing a baseball at full speed to test the strength of the repaired tendon'],'B','The repair needs time to heal before active motion and strengthening begin.',PAG),
  q(MSK,'What does “weight bearing as tolerated” (WBAT) mean?','The patient may put as much weight on the leg as comfort allows',['No weight on the leg at all','Only the toes may touch the floor for balance while hopping','Full weight must be used even if very painful'],'C','The patient’s own pain guides how much weight is placed on the leg.',PAG),
  q(MSK,'A patient with low back pain reports numbness in the groin and new bladder problems. What is suspected?','Cauda equina syndrome, which needs emergency referral',['Simple muscle strain','Normal soreness after exercise','A mild ankle sprain'],'D','Pressure on the nerve roots at the end of the spinal cord can cause permanent damage.',PAG),
  q(MSK,'Pain on the inside of the elbow with resisted wrist flexion suggests which condition?','Medial epicondylitis (golfer’s elbow)',['Lateral epicondylitis, which hurts on the outside of the elbow with wrist extension','Carpal tunnel syndrome','A biceps tendon rupture'],'A','Repeated gripping and wrist bending overload the tendons at the inner elbow.',PAG),
  q(MSK,'Which exercise is commonly used to treat Achilles tendinopathy?','Eccentric heel drops off a step',['Complete rest in a cast for 3 months','Jumping on the toes as high as possible','Ice only'],'B','Slow lowering of the heel loads the tendon and helps it remodel.',PAG),
  q(MSK,'An older adult’s leg pain with walking improves when leaning forward on a shopping cart. What is likely?','Lumbar spinal stenosis',['Plantar fasciitis','An ankle sprain','A rotator cuff tear that causes the arm to feel weak when reaching overhead'],'C','Bending forward opens the spinal canal and relieves pressure on the nerves.',PAG),
  q(MSK,'Which treatment is most important for knee osteoarthritis?','Strengthening the quadriceps and staying active',['Strict bed rest','Avoiding all walking','Wearing high heels to shift the weight off the inner part of the knee joint'],'D','Strong thigh muscles absorb force and reduce stress on the knee joint.',PAG),
  q(NEURO,'After a neck injury, a patient’s arms are much weaker than the legs. Which syndrome is likely?','Central cord syndrome',['Brown-Séquard syndrome, which affects one side of the body more than the other','Cauda equina syndrome','Bell palsy'],'A','It often happens in older adults after a fall that forces the neck backward.',PAG),
  q(NEURO,'After a stroke, a patient cannot see the left half of the visual field in both eyes. What is this called?','Homonymous hemianopsia',['Nystagmus','Cataract','Unilateral neglect, where the patient ignores one side even though they can see it'],'B','Teaching the patient to turn the head to scan helps make up for the lost field.',PAG),
  q(NEURO,'A polio survivor has new fatigue and weakness decades later. Which approach is most helpful?','Energy conservation and avoiding overwork of weak muscles',['Maximum-effort lifting every day','Complete bed rest','High-intensity exercise to exhaustion'],'C','Post-polio syndrome is managed by pacing activities and preventing overuse.',PAG),
  q(NEURO,'Which scale is commonly used to grade spasticity?','The Modified Ashworth Scale',['The Braden Scale','The Borg scale','The Glasgow Coma Scale, which scores eye, verbal and motor responses'],'D','Scores range from 0 (no increase in tone) to 4 (limb rigid in flexion or extension).',PAG),
  q(NEURO,'How should a caregiver help a stroke survivor with a flaccid, partly dislocated shoulder?','Support the arm and avoid pulling on it during transfers',['Lift the patient by the weak arm','Let the arm hang freely all day','Swing the arm in large circles to loosen it up before each meal and transfer'],'A','Pulling on the weak arm can cause pain and further joint injury.',PAG),
  q(NEURO,'Which condition causes burning pain, swelling and skin color changes after a minor injury?','Complex regional pain syndrome',['Multiple sclerosis','Osteoarthritis','Parkinson disease, which causes tremor at rest and slow movement'],'B','Early gentle movement and desensitization are key parts of treatment.',PAG),
  q(NEURO,'Which finding points to a lower motor neuron injury?','Flaccid weakness with muscle wasting and reduced reflexes',['Spasticity with increased reflexes','A positive Babinski sign','Clonus at the ankle and a stiff, scissoring walking pattern with legs crossing'],'C','Upper motor neuron injuries cause spasticity and brisk reflexes instead.',PAG),
  q(NEURO,'Which inherited nerve disorder often causes high foot arches, foot drop and weak lower legs?','Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease',['Myasthenia gravis','Bell palsy','Huntington disease, which causes jerky movements called chorea'],'D','Ankle-foot orthoses and stretching help with walking and foot problems.',PAG),
  q(CP,'Why may heart rate not rise normally during exercise in a patient taking a beta-blocker?','The drug slows the heart’s response, so RPE is used to guide intensity',['Beta-blockers make the heart beat faster','Beta-blockers have no effect on the heart','Heart rate cannot be measured'],'A','The Borg scale helps keep exercise at a safe level when heart rate is blunted.',PF),
  q(CP,'After open-heart surgery, which sternal precaution is common?','Avoid pushing up with the arms or lifting heavy objects',['Lift as much weight as possible with both arms','Do push-ups daily','Sleep on the stomach every night to keep the healing chest flat and stable'],'B','Protecting the breastbone allows it to heal after surgery, which usually takes 6 to 8 weeks.',PF),
  q(CP,'A patient has new calf pain, warmth and swelling in one leg. What should the PT do?','Hold exercise and notify the medical team about a possible DVT',['Massage the calf firmly','Have the patient walk faster','Apply a heat pack to the calf and continue with the planned leg exercises as usual'],'C','Massaging or exercising the leg could move a clot to the lungs.',PF),
  q(CP,'Which phase of cardiac rehab is long-term maintenance, often done in a community setting?','Phase III or IV',['Phase I, which starts in the hospital','Phase II','Phase 0'],'D','Patients continue exercising on their own to keep the gains from earlier phases.',PF),
  q(CP,'Which rating scale is commonly used to measure shortness of breath during exercise in COPD?','The modified Borg dyspnea scale (0–10)',['The Glasgow Coma Scale','The Braden Scale','The Berg Balance Scale, which measures balance through 14 tasks'],'A','Patients rate their breathlessness so exercise can be adjusted safely.',PF),
  q(CP,'Why is interval training often used for patients with COPD?','Short bursts with rest allow more total exercise with less breathlessness',['It requires no rest at all','It is the only type of exercise allowed in any lung disease','It replaces all breathing exercises and medicines the patient takes at home'],'B','Interval training lets patients work harder for short periods than continuous exercise allows.',PF),
  q(CP,'What is a normal resting SpO₂ for a healthy adult?','95% to 100%',['70% to 80%','50% to 60%','About 88%, the level many COPD patients are kept above'],'C','Readings below 90% are a concern in most patients without chronic lung disease.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'Using the rule of nines, about what percentage is the entire front of the trunk in an adult?','18%',['9%, the same as one arm','1%','36%'],'D','The back of the trunk is also 18%, and each leg is 18%, which helps estimate burn size.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'Which scar grows beyond the edges of the original wound?','A keloid',['A hypertrophic scar, which stays within the wound','A flat mature scar','A stage 1 pressure injury'],'A','Keloids are more common in people with darker skin and may come back after removal.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'After a new skin graft, what is usually done with the area for the first several days?','Keep it still so the graft can attach',['Move it through full range of motion right away','Apply heavy massage','Soak it in a hot whirlpool for 30 minutes twice a day to keep the graft clean and moist'],'B','Movement can shift the graft and keep it from taking, so splints are often used.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'Which surface helps prevent pressure injuries in a patient at high risk who is in bed most of the day?','A low-air-loss or pressure-redistribution mattress',['A firm wooden board','A standard foam pillow under the heels only','A plastic sheet'],'C','Special surfaces spread out pressure and reduce moisture against the skin.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'A wound edge is rolled under and thick. What is this called?','Epibole',['Granulation','Maceration','Eschar, which is dry, black dead tissue covering the wound bed'],'D','Rolled edges stop new skin cells from moving across the wound and may need treatment.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'How are the heels best protected from pressure in a bedbound patient?','Float the heels off the bed with a pillow under the calves',['Place a pillow directly under the heels','Keep the patient’s heels on a firm surface','Raise the foot of the bed so the patient slides down toward it'],'A','Floating removes all pressure from the heels, which are a common site for injury.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'Which dressing is often used for shallow wounds with light drainage?','A hydrocolloid dressing',['A dry gauze packing changed every hour','No dressing at all','A heavy foam dressing for wounds with large amounts of drainage'],'B','Hydrocolloids keep the wound moist and can stay on for several days.',PF),
  q(AGE,'What does the Gross Motor Function Classification System (GMFCS) describe?','The level of movement ability in children with cerebral palsy',['A child’s reading level','A child’s height','How fast a child can run compared with others the same age in a school race'],'C','Levels range from I (walks without limits) to V (needs a wheelchair and full help).',PAG),
  q(AGE,'At about what age do most children hop on one foot?','About 4 years',['About 6 months','About 12 months','About 10 years, after most children ride a bike without training wheels'],'D','Hopping requires balance and strength that develop during the preschool years.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'Which condition causes weakness from loss of motor neurons in the spinal cord in infants and children?','Spinal muscular atrophy',['Down syndrome','Clubfoot','Hip dysplasia, in which the hip joint is loose or out of place at birth'],'A','New gene-based treatments have greatly improved outcomes for some children.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'An infant has a flat spot on the back of the head. Which advice helps?','Supervised tummy time while awake and changing head position',['Keeping the baby on the back all day while awake','Using a pillow in the crib','Face-down sleeping at night'],'B','Babies should sleep on their backs, but awake tummy time builds strength and helps head shape.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'When is walking usually started after hip fracture surgery in an older adult?','Within about 1 to 2 days, if the patient is stable',['After 6 weeks of bed rest','Never; patients use a wheelchair for life','After 3 months of bed rest to let the bone fully heal before putting any weight on it'],'C','Early walking lowers the risk of pneumonia, clots and loss of strength.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'How does maximum heart rate usually change with age?','It decreases',['It increases','It stays exactly the same from childhood to old age','It doubles after age 65'],'D','Exercise targets for older adults are adjusted for this natural decline.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'Which home change most reduces fall risk for an older adult?','Removing loose throw rugs and adding grab bars',['Adding more loose rugs for padding','Turning off night lights to save energy','Moving often-used items to the highest shelves so they are out of the way'],'A','Good lighting and clear walkways are also simple but effective changes.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'A 4-year-old still walks on the toes all the time with no known cause. What is this called?','Idiopathic toe walking',['Clubfoot','Normal walking for every child at all ages','Hip dysplasia'],'B','Stretching, casting or bracing may be used to lengthen the tight calf muscles.',PAG)
